#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111604} :activity/id #uuid "78489629-e548-5866-8b96-e96990be5e28" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048295 :topic/id #uuid "920e0459-5102-56be-8b6e-f637c62f1366" :topic/math-academy-id 1531} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111827 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=0.5^x$. First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y = 0.5^x$ | $8$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ | $0.125$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/b1/b17b1e0f554b8da98f46b89265cbec3be2fab1a40b6c04ef03f5b4cec94ac3d8.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$.\n2. The graph is decreasing.\n3. For $x<0$, the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ decreases. Symbolically, we can write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n4. For $x>0$, the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ increases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has a horizontal asymptote $y=0$. Symbolically, we can write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n5. The domain is $x\\in\\left(-\\infty, \\infty\\right)$, and the range is $y>0$.\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $0 < a < 1$. These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/6c/6c5e94fdb76c1af54dcd1cd8d44bb532ab5c69a305465282b62c28ef2b54ab75.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e919c9a-e896-5b0b-8570-5a7a377146d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1544 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fa05b5ee-1d45-5481-a6c9-e019dfaa173b" :step/math-academy-id 3935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110313 :question/id #uuid "c02ce0b7-d660-5d31-8147-303a9e6f806d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1223"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329831}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"} :step/id #uuid "41927360-32e5-5db6-a9f3-26f71dd06db5" :step/math-academy-id 3936 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111828 :tutorial/content "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve\n\n$$\ny=2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nby a factor of $2$.\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nand multiplies them by $2$. This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/36/3654463dd3914bb65ffe58be20cdf2bc4cd19e5a51ac479f4aaca50e2709a214.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$.\n\nOn the other hand, the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\left(2^x\\right)\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n$y = 2^x$\nby a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y = 2^x$ and multiplies them by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nThis has the effect of **compressing** the curve downwards.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched or compressed exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "4ea28190-d93f-521e-964b-cdd4aad84f69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1545 :tutorial/title "Vertical Stretching and Compression"} :step/id #uuid "04a9b12e-b969-57f1-a5e7-19bc2fc561a7" :step/math-academy-id 3939 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110668 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110314 :question/id #uuid "90054a4d-adcf-56b8-ab73-86048bc0f4d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1224"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46b4837f-6ba5-5812-8dc7-4671fcd6b20d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390340}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Scaled Exponential Decay Function"} :step/id #uuid "adbf259f-fa2f-5745-bd7e-7048bd565045" :step/math-academy-id 3940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110315 :question/id #uuid "055bdf27-d354-57d7-96a6-de3300053035" :question/math-academy-id "e-1225"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339121}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "9dfe9d61-57bc-5cba-b15e-a9df1319f56e" :step/math-academy-id 3943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110370 :question/id #uuid "edf79f97-9325-55a0-a78b-133bba524177" :question/math-academy-id "e-1803"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41d2e6d0-bfad-5fe0-a055-aaa9ed9ae31c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111140}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of an Exponential Decay Function"} :step/id #uuid "2cc2ead9-f661-50ca-96a5-5747e77f1192" :step/math-academy-id 6235 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111605}}] :activity/title "Graphing Exponential Decay Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110876}]}
